On 1 Nov 2016, at 22:42, Meredith Peterson <smer....@gmail.com> wrote:

> I've had success skeletonizing and 3d plotting my nrrd files as dotprops objects,

Great.

> but I've run into issues trying to achieve the following:
>
> 1) visualizing the intersect of multiple GAL4 expression patterns in 3D space, superimposed on a surface plot of the ventral nervous system standard

You could try using functions like prune to do this if you can find a distance threshold within which all neurites nust reside.

http://jefferis.github.io/nat/reference/prune.html

> 2) determining whether any of these intersections can be assigned to distinct tracts or commissures in the VNS

Do you have set of traced tracts? How will you know when you have a match? If you have tract tracings you can nblast them against your patterns. If you only have eg an nc82 stained stack you will have to compare that in some 3D visualisation software. We use amira.

> This seemed pretty straightforward at first but with one complication: I am not generating single-cell clones, so the dotprops objects will represent multiple neurons.

That's one of the use cases for the flexible dotprops format

> Additionally, raster 3d objects (like im3ds) seem like a better choice for generating the sort of '3d heatmaps' I'd like to get, but as far as I can tell the intersect functions can only handle vector objects.

I'm not sure which intersect functions you are referring to here. You can do logical operations on 3D volumes like a pair of im3d objects to ask which voxels are positive in both voxels. but what you have to be careful about is the spatial precision of your expression patterns after registration. People normally address this by smoothing their data with eg a Gaussian of sd ~3 microns.

